\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Example}
\title{The real Clifford algebra of signature (1,3)}
\lean/tauceti{TauCeti.realClifford_classification_of_residue_eq_two}

\p{Use the convention of \ref{fcap-0011}: #{p} counts positive squares,
#{q} counts negative squares, and a Clifford generator obeys
#{v^2=Q(v)1}. Then the real algebra for one positive and three negative
axes is

##{\Cl_{1,3}\simeq M_2(\mathbb H).}

The Tau Ceti classification theorem states existence of an abstract real
algebra equivalence. Here #{q-p=2} is its residue modulo #{8}, and its
matrix size is #{2^{(p+q-2)/2}=2}.}

\p{\citet{II.2.9, pp. 65--66}{chevalley1954algebraic} uses the same
positive/negative index convention. Split off one hyperbolic plane of
signature #{(1,1)}; the orthogonal remainder is a negative plane of
signature #{(0,2)}. Chevalley's dimension-two definite calculation on
p. 66 identifies its Clifford algebra with #{\mathbb H}. The
hyperbolic-plane theorem
\citet{II.2.1, pp. 42--43}{chevalley1954algebraic} gives
#{\Cl_{1,1}\simeq M_2(\mathbb R)}.}

\p{\citet{II.2.5, pp. 45--46}{chevalley1954algebraic} gives an
orthogonal-sum tensor-product formula for an even-dimensional
nondegenerate first summand; the second quadratic form is twisted.
Here normalized hyperbolic generators #{e^2=1} and #{f^2=-1} have
volume #{J=ef} with #{J^2=1}. For a vector #{v} in the orthogonal
remainder, #{Jv} commutes with #{e,f} and still squares to #{Q(v)}.
Since #{v=J(Jv)}, these commuting factors generate the full algebra.
Thus the special hyperbolic split leaves that remainder unchanged:

##{\begin{aligned}
\Cl_{1,3}&\simeq\Cl_{1,1}\otimes_{\mathbb R}\Cl_{0,2}\\
&\simeq M_2(\mathbb R)\otimes_{\mathbb R}\mathbb H
\simeq M_2(\mathbb H).
\end{aligned}}

Both sides have real dimension #{16=2^4=4\dim_{\mathbb R}\mathbb H}.
By comparison, #{M_4(\mathbb C)} has real dimension #{32}. This
abstract classification does not identify any particular gamma-matrix
map, its kernel, or its image.}
